# Code conventions

## Context

The seeded `app/` is TypeScript strict (`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` on) and
consistent about a few conventions. Existing files (`app/src/actions.ts`,
`app/src/reducer.ts`, `app/src/selectors.ts`) all use named exports and immutable
updates — new code must match so the codebase stays uniform and type-safe.

## Rule

- Use named exports ONLY. No `export default`.
- No `any` and no `@ts-ignore` / `@ts-expect-error`. Model types precisely; if a
  type is missing, add it to `app/src/types.ts` (see the do-not-touch rule).
- Update state immutably — spread objects/arrays, `map`, `filter`. Never mutate
  in place (`state.tasks.push(...)`, `task.done = ...`).
- Filenames are kebab-case (e.g. `lib/text.ts`); types/interfaces are PascalCase
  (`AppState`, `Task`, `Action`); functions/vars are camelCase.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rnE "export default|: any|@ts-(ignore|expect-error)" app/src` → zero.
2. `grep -rnE "\.push\(|\.splice\(|\.(done|title|filter) =" app/src` → no direct
   mutation of state.
3. `cd app && npm run typecheck` passes.
